1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,762
Circularity, commonly known as double
dipping, occurs when data selection

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:09,047
criteria are not independent of the
subsequent statistical test.

3
00:00:09,047 --> 00:00:15,714
If you search the whole brain for voxels
showing greater activation for task over

4
00:00:15,714 --> 00:00:21,904
baseline, and then extract and report the
mean activation from those exact voxels,

5
00:00:21,904 --> 00:00:26,190
your effect sizes and p-values will be
artificially inflated.

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:34,444
You can prevent circularity through four
standard strategies: define regions using

7
00:00:34,444 --> 00:00:40,666
anatomical atlases; run an independent
localizer scan; use orthogonal contrasts

8
00:00:40,666 --> 00:00:46,266
where selection and testing are
mathematically uncorrelated; or apply

9
00:00:46,266 --> 00:00:47,510
leave-one-subject-out cross-validation.

10
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:55,097
Never perform hypothesis tests on voxels
selected with that same test.

11
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:59,867
Always derive region boundaries
independently to ensure your reported

12
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:02,517
effect sizes are scientifically
defensible.
